  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca-tangeri-2/",
      "html": "<p>The eastern Atlantic from Portugal to Angola is the origin of the fiddler crab Uca tangeri, where it inhabits sand and mangrove coasts. The animals are obtained from Nigeria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca-tangeri-2/",
      "html": "<p>In their periodically flooded habitat they inhabit muddy burrows, which they leave at ebb-tide for the intake of food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca-tangeri-2/",
      "html": "<p>For maintenance it should be tried to imitate their habitat, i.e. a land part with sand and gravel and a water part filled with brackish water should be present in the aquarium. Some hiding places give them retreat possibilities.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca-tangeri-2/",
      "html": "<p>As omnivores they are not discriminating anything and feed on nearly each organic matter the sea is offering them. With the domestic care, frosted and granulated food can be fed, as well as different fruits and vegetables.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca-tangeri-2/",
      "html": "<p>The to an enormous size grown major claw of the males is remarkable. It is used for attracting the females and for the fight between rivaling males.</p>"
    }
  ],
